1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,270
THEME SONG: In the not
 too distant future--

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:09,430
next Sunday AD-- there
 was a guy named Joel,

3
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:12,620
not too different
 from you or me.

4
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:17,210
He worked at Gizmonic
 Institute, just another face

5
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:21,930
in a red jumpsuit, he did a
 good job cleaning up the place,

6
00:00:21,930 --> 00:00:25,340
but his bosses didn't like him,
 so they shot him into space.

7
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:34,360
We'll send him cheesy movies,
 the worst we can find.

8
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:37,660
He'll have to sit
 and watch them all,

9
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:40,440
and we'll monitor his mind.

10
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:43,580
Now keep in mind,
 Joel can't control

11
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:47,860
where the movies begin
 or end, because he

12
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:52,430
used those special parts
 to make his robot friends.

13
00:00:55,310 --> 00:00:58,180
Robot Roll Call-- Cambot!

14
00:00:58,180 --> 00:00:59,150
Gypsy!

15
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:01,040
Tom Servo!

16
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:02,410
Croooow!

17
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:05,630
If you're wondering how
 he eats and breathes

18
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:10,020
and other science facts,
 then repeat to yourself,

19
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:13,450
it's just a show, I
 should really just

20
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:31,170
relax for Mystery
 Science Theater 3000!

21
00:01:31,170 --> 00:01:32,770
-Crow, look, here he comes!

22
00:01:32,770 --> 00:01:33,960
It's Joel!

23
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:34,460
-Joel!

24
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:35,440
It's Joel, Joel, Joel!

25
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:37,050
[CHEERING]

26
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:37,710
-Hi, you guys.

27
00:01:37,710 --> 00:01:38,790
How you doing?

28
00:01:38,790 --> 00:01:40,370
-Oh, you know,
 it's just like you

29
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:42,250
to think of us
 instead of yourself.

30
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:44,410
-That's what separates
 you from us measly bots.

31
00:01:44,410 --> 00:01:44,910
-Thanks.

32
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:45,910
Hi, everybody.

33
00:01:45,910 --> 00:01:47,240
Welcome to the
 Satellite of Love.

34
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:48,580
I'm Joel Robinson
 and these are--

35
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:49,190
-Oh, ya see?

36
00:01:49,190 --> 00:01:50,120
He introduced himself.

37
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:52,100
What a perfectly
 thoughtful thing to do.

38
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:52,850
-Oh yes, thoughtful.

39
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:54,620
-These are my robots
 Tom Servo and Crow.

40
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:56,910
And I've installed in them
 a protocol module that

41
00:01:56,910 --> 00:01:58,960
makes them believe
 everything I think, say or do

42
00:01:58,960 --> 00:01:59,860
is utterly brilliant.

43
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:01,050
-Oh, and it is, Joel.

44
00:02:01,050 --> 00:02:01,730
God, yes.

45
00:02:01,730 --> 00:02:02,260
Oh ho ho, joy!

46
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:04,750
-Roger Penrose on his best
 day isn't half as brilliant

47
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:06,720
or as charming as
 you, I might add.

48
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:08,860
-Commercial sign in 15 seconds.

49
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:11,110
Of course, every
 blissful second with you

50
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:13,040
is like an apple of gold, Joel.

51
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:13,540
-Ah!

52
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:14,710
-Yes, yes, yes, yes, yes.

53
00:02:14,710 --> 00:02:16,740
-You know, I'm starting to have
 second thoughts about this.

54
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:17,980
I don't know what I think.

55
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:18,950
-Oh, Joel!

56
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:21,010
I've wiped away every
 bit of hesitation

57
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:22,020
I had about this project.

58
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:23,880
-Not that we'd take issue
 with anything you'd think.

59
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:24,450
-Oh, no no no no.

60
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:25,420
-No no no.

61
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:27,240
-It does make it
 easier to work with.

62
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:29,110
-Oh yes yes, easy as pie!

63
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:30,010
Brilliant, brilliant.

64
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:31,080
-More Evian, Mr. Miller?

65
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:33,040
-It leaves me feeling
 kind of hollow.

66
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:33,610
-Wow.

67
00:02:33,610 --> 00:02:35,870
How insightful of you to
 find that in yourself.

68
00:02:35,870 --> 00:02:38,280
-You always cut right to
 the heart of things, Joel.

69
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:38,780
-Ah, yeah.

70
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:40,440
-Yeah, I'm going to
 remove those modules.

71
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:40,940
-Oh, no no.

72
00:02:40,940 --> 00:02:43,230
No, just, just, just, just
 shut me down completely.

73
00:02:43,230 --> 00:02:43,740
-Oh!

74
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:45,580
Uh, jettison me
 into space, please!

75
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:47,650
-Oh, yes, disconnect
 with extreme prejudice.

76
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:48,720
-You see this butt?

77
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:49,570
Kick this butt.

78
00:02:49,570 --> 00:02:50,410
-We'll be right back.

79
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:58,570
All right, there's
 the protocol module.

80
00:02:58,570 --> 00:02:59,720
-Don't touch me.

81
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:00,890
Don't ever touch me.

82
00:03:00,890 --> 00:03:01,460
-This is painful.

83
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:02,170
That-- ah!

84
00:03:02,170 --> 00:03:02,670
Ungh.

85
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:03,540
[SNIFFING]

86
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:04,490
-Jeez, Joel.

87
00:03:04,490 --> 00:03:07,200
Some small animal crawl
 inside your jumpsuit and die?

88
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,470
-There's the bots
 I know and love.

89
00:03:09,470 --> 00:03:10,860
Uh-oh, fasten your seatbelts.

90
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:12,780
Crate &amp; Barrel are calling.

91
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:15,150
[HUMMING_"LA_MARSEILLAISE"]

92
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:16,130
-Huh?

93
00:03:16,130 --> 00:03:17,380
Oh, Joel.

94
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:19,900
A hearty hello to you and yours.

95
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:22,880
Let me just say this about
 today's invention exchange--

96
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:24,690
let them eat chocolate.

97
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:28,260
-That's right, J--
 That's right, Joel.

98
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:31,940
Our invention this week is based
 on one's natural inclination

99
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:34,570
to bite the heads off
 of chocolate bunnies.

100
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:35,740
-That's right, Frank.

101
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:39,150
That's why we've invented the
 chocolate bunny guillotine.

102
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:43,340
Eliminate the guesswork in
 biting the heads off bunnies.

103
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:45,290
Uh, ready, Mr. Executioner?

104
00:03:45,290 --> 00:03:48,390
-Yes, my liege.

105
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:51,970
-You have stolen painted
 eggs in a time of famine.

106
00:03:51,970 --> 00:03:53,290
Uh, off with her head, Frank.

107
00:03:56,050 --> 00:03:57,950
And no chocolate mess.

108
00:03:57,950 --> 00:03:59,570
Well, poopsies?

109
00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:00,070
[BOTS CRYING]

110
00:04:00,070 --> 00:04:03,080
-What about the pardon
 from Fanny Farmer?

111
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:03,770
-Yeah.

112
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:06,820
His only crime was
 being born delicious!

113
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:08,020
-It's OK, you guys.

114
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:08,760
There, there.

115
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:09,310
It's all right.

116
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:11,970
Let's show them our invention
 exchange, all right?

117
00:04:11,970 --> 00:04:14,570
Our invention exchange
 is called the Cartuner.

118
00:04:14,570 --> 00:04:16,670
Have you ever noticed
 that funnies just

119
00:04:16,670 --> 00:04:18,240
aren't funny anymore?

120
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:20,960
The Cartuner takes
 stodgy, ambiguous cartoons

121
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:24,060
like Mark Trail and mixes
 them with stiflingly unfunny

122
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:27,630
cartoons like Blondie, puts them
 together and makes them funny.

123
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:29,150
Check it out.

124
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:30,120
Well, look at this.

125
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:30,620
Huh.

126
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:31,460
Let's see what happened.

127
00:04:31,460 --> 00:04:32,610
Well, OK.

128
00:04:32,610 --> 00:04:36,010
And here's Dagwood, and he's
 eating a giant sandwich made

129
00:04:36,010 --> 00:04:38,380
out of twigs, grubs,
 and a tufted titmouse.

130
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:39,240
[BOTS LAUGHING]

131
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:40,120
-That's pretty funny.

132
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:40,990
-Hey, that's funny.

133
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:41,490
-Oh, oh, oh!

134
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:43,070
Now do the Lockhorns and Cathy.

135
00:04:43,070 --> 00:04:43,570
-Ooh, yeah.

136
00:04:43,570 --> 00:04:44,070
Yeah, yeah.

137
00:04:44,070 --> 00:04:45,070
-OK, Lockhorns and Cathy.

138
00:04:45,070 --> 00:04:46,620
Put them in the Cartuner.

139
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:47,550
-It goes round and round.

140
00:04:47,550 --> 00:04:49,880
-Yes, and it comes out here.

141
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:50,830
-Ah, let me see.

142
00:04:50,830 --> 00:04:52,370
Ah-- Oh ho, hey.

143
00:04:52,370 --> 00:04:55,070
Mrs. Lockhorn accidentally
 crashed her car into the store

144
00:04:55,070 --> 00:04:57,150
where Cathy was
 trying on a swimsuit!

145
00:04:57,150 --> 00:05:00,720
[LAUGHTER]

146
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:01,220
-OK.

147
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:01,720
-Hey, Joel.

148
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:04,470
Uh, let's try a panel of Bil
 Keane's heartwarming Family

149
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:07,010
Circus with Gary Larson's
 off-center The Far Side.

150
00:05:07,010 --> 00:05:11,170
-OK, put them together, mix them
 up, and we can-- and then, see,

151
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:12,370
here it is.

152
00:05:12,370 --> 00:05:12,870
Huh.

153
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:13,600
-Oh, let me see there.

154
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:17,110
A dotted trail showing where
 anthropomorphic jackals have

155
00:05:17,110 --> 00:05:18,060
chased little Billy!

156
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,010
-It's a hoot!

157
00:05:19,010 --> 00:05:20,440
[LAUGHING]

158
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:21,390
-Ah, the carnage!

159
00:05:21,390 --> 00:05:22,260
-What do you think, sirs?

160
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:24,280
-Ahh-- my ribs.

161
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:26,530
-I don't think, I get even.

162
00:05:26,530 --> 00:05:28,350
Dig this, mes amis.

163
00:05:28,350 --> 00:05:33,120
Your average Joe has never seen
 a film like today's experiment.

164
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:35,210
Your average person
 on the street

165
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:37,940
has not even begun to
 conceptualize the horror which

166
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:43,350
is your experiment
 today, Mr. Joel Average.

167
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:47,110
I give to you, "Manos--
 The Hands of Fate."

168
00:05:47,110 --> 00:05:50,770
Served with a short, the
 stirring conclusion to "Hired."

169
00:05:50,770 --> 00:05:51,540
Hit the button, Frank.

170
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:52,040
-Yes.

171
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:53,200
Oh, Fluffy, you're so--

172
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:53,880
-Frank!

173
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:56,080
--Oh, Fluffy, here.

174
00:05:56,080 --> 00:05:57,810
Here, you take him.

175
00:06:02,950 --> 00:06:04,620
-Push the button, Frank!

176
00:06:04,620 --> 00:06:07,190
-Ziggy had Garfield neutered?

177
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:09,440
Now that's funny!

178
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:11,410
[BUZZER ALARM]

179
00:06:25,250 --> 00:06:26,730
-Ah, look.

180
00:06:26,730 --> 00:06:29,540
"Hired 2-- Electric Boogaloo."

181
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:31,490
-A Jam Handy to the rescue.

182
00:06:31,490 --> 00:06:34,190
-My Jam Handy.

183
00:06:34,190 --> 00:06:38,650
-No employees were killed
 during the making of this film.

184
00:06:38,650 --> 00:06:41,890
-Young fellas today won't work
 the way we did 11 years ago.

185
00:06:41,890 --> 00:06:43,230
-Previously, on "Hired."

186
00:06:43,230 --> 00:06:44,650
-Maybe not, son.

187
00:06:44,650 --> 00:06:48,200
And then again, maybe they're
 working harder than you think.

188
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:50,490
-I may be wonderful but
 I think you're wrong.

189
00:06:50,490 --> 00:06:52,320
-You were a pretty
 good man when you first

190
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:53,730
started in this business.

191
00:06:53,730 --> 00:06:58,690
And you probably were, but
 you owe a lot of your success

192
00:06:58,690 --> 00:07:01,320
to the way Harry
 Carpenter worked with you

193
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:03,910
-Give 'em hell, Harry.

194
00:07:03,910 --> 00:07:07,140
-He used to tell me about
 the mistakes you made.

195
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:09,520
You may not have known
 it, but you really

196
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:11,640
served am apprenticeship.

197
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:14,980
-Work, booze, work--

198
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:16,800
-In every business.

199
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:18,770
-Yeah, I suppose so.

200
00:07:18,770 --> 00:07:21,670
-Remember how closely he
 worked with you the first year?

201
00:07:21,670 --> 00:07:22,170
Heh.

202
00:07:22,170 --> 00:07:23,570
He used to tell me about it.

203
00:07:23,570 --> 00:07:24,800
-He ripped you a
 new one everyday.

204
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:27,760
-Yeah, I remember how he used to
 talk to me about my prospects.

205
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:28,340
-Your prostate?

206
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:30,970
-How I was to
 handle some of them.

207
00:07:30,970 --> 00:07:32,300
But you know, I always
 thought that that

208
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:34,740
was because he knew you so well.

209
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:37,520
Maybe he had more of a
 personal interest in me.

210
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:38,420
-Nonsense.

211
00:07:38,420 --> 00:07:39,790
-He hated you.

212
00:07:39,790 --> 00:07:40,830
-Not Harry.

213
00:07:40,830 --> 00:07:42,310
-Oh, he's having an episode.

214
00:07:42,310 --> 00:07:45,100
-He worked that hard with
 every man he ever hired.

215
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:46,170
-He got sent to jail for it.

216
00:07:46,170 --> 00:07:48,560
-He was smart enough to
 realize that he knew more

217
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:52,660
about selling cars than
 any man on his sales force.

218
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:55,340
And that it took his
 direction and leadership

219
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:57,180
to get the most out of his men.

220
00:07:57,180 --> 00:07:57,680
-Ah!

221
00:07:57,680 --> 00:07:58,790
Flying elves are back.

222
00:07:58,790 --> 00:08:02,830
-And I don't believe men
 have changed much since then.

223
00:08:02,830 --> 00:08:03,560
-Aah!

224
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:05,920
-How about this Jimmy?

225
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:06,850
Doesn't he work?

226
00:08:06,850 --> 00:08:09,550
Doesn't he get in for the
 morning sales meetings?

227
00:08:09,550 --> 00:08:10,940
[HICCUP]

228
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:12,220
-Oh yeah, he's always punctual.

229
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:14,450
He never misses.

230
00:08:14,450 --> 00:08:15,790
But take his
 prospects, for example.

231
00:08:15,790 --> 00:08:16,430
-Please.

232
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:17,030
[IMITATES RIMSHOT]

233
00:08:17,030 --> 00:08:18,330
-Why, I looked them
 over the other day,

234
00:08:18,330 --> 00:08:21,120
and it's just about the
 poorest list I've ever seen.

235
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:22,390
I remember one of
 the first things

236
00:08:22,390 --> 00:08:23,660
that Harry drilled in to me--

237
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:24,380
-Was Harry!

238
00:08:24,380 --> 00:08:26,440
-The importance of having
 a good list of prospects.

239
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:27,510
-Yes?

240
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:29,540
-Then he showed me how
 to go about getting it.

241
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:31,130
How to keep it up.

242
00:08:31,130 --> 00:08:32,100
How to cover the list.

243
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:32,820
And how often.

244
00:08:32,820 --> 00:08:34,410
-Yeah, he performed
 puppet shows for me.

245
00:08:34,410 --> 00:08:36,300
-I had the importance
 of prospects drilled

246
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:37,740
into me from the
 very first day I

247
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:38,330
-Whoa!

248
00:08:38,330 --> 00:08:38,830
-Aah!

249
00:08:38,830 --> 00:08:39,500
-He's evil!

250
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:40,480
-Mhm.

251
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:42,190
-Has Jimmy?

252
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:44,500
-Has Jimmy what?

253
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:48,260
-Had the importance of
 prospects pounded into him

254
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:50,830
since the first day
 he started to work?

255
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:52,680
-Well, I've told him
 how important they are.

256
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:55,020
-Yeah, but have
 you shown him how,

257
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:56,580
when and where to get them?

258
00:08:56,580 --> 00:08:58,470
Have you checked
 on him every day

259
00:08:58,470 --> 00:08:59,880
to see that he did it right?

260
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:00,660
-Have you hugged him?

261
00:09:00,660 --> 00:09:02,350
-Dad, I've got other work to do.

262
00:09:02,350 --> 00:09:05,270
-Harry wasn't too busy to
 make a good man out of you.

263
00:09:05,270 --> 00:09:09,070
-Ah, you still got
 a little-- eh-- hmm.

264
00:09:09,070 --> 00:09:11,680
-Say, I think I'm beginning
 to see what you mean.

265
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:14,070
-I'm beginning to sober
 up and you're scaring me.

266
00:09:14,070 --> 00:09:14,750
-I wonder.

267
00:09:14,750 --> 00:09:17,430
-(SINGING) I wah,
 wah, wah, wah, wonder.

268
00:09:17,430 --> 00:09:18,760
-Say, maybe that's
 the reason I haven't

269
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:20,190
had very much luck with Jimmy.

270
00:09:20,190 --> 00:09:20,940
-I'm gonna dance!

271
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:24,360
-Or with Henderson, that other
 fellow I hired last year.

272
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:25,790
You know, he looked
 pretty good to me, too,

273
00:09:25,790 --> 00:09:27,050
Dad, when I first hired him.

274
00:09:27,050 --> 00:09:27,770
-Heh, who doesn't?

275
00:09:27,770 --> 00:09:30,470
-Certainly would be worth a try.

276
00:09:30,470 --> 00:09:31,160
-Yes, sir.

277
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:31,860
-Don't hit me!

278
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:33,830
-Come to think of it, the time
 I'm spending on other things

279
00:09:33,830 --> 00:09:36,740
right now might be costing me
 a whole lot more than I think.

280
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:38,280
Men need leadership.

281
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:39,620
Leadership is important.

282
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:41,720
Oh, I've always known
 that, Dad, but I never

283
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:44,050
thought about in connection
 with my own business.

284
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:45,370
But starting Monday morning,

285
00:09:45,370 --> 00:09:46,220
-Don't touch me!

286
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:47,680
-I'm gonna stir things up a bit.

287
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:50,380
I'm going to show these fellas
 what leadership really means.

288
00:09:50,380 --> 00:09:50,980
[HICCUP]

289
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:53,720
-And then we'll see.

290
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:56,190
-We're going to have leadership
 the way my old man told me.

291
00:09:56,190 --> 00:09:57,770
You, put a handkerchief
 on your head!

292
00:09:57,770 --> 00:09:59,570
You, swat at imaginary elves!

293
00:09:59,570 --> 00:10:03,260
You, rock on the
 porch all night!

294
00:10:03,260 --> 00:10:05,400
-Sales are the most important
 thing in this business.

295
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:06,670
-Seeing as how we're
 salesmen and all.

296
00:10:06,670 --> 00:10:08,140
-We're going to work
 closer together than we

297
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:09,830
have in the past in
 order to get more sales.

298
00:10:09,830 --> 00:10:11,040
-But first, martinis!

299
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:12,210
-Well, that's all
 for this meeting,

300
00:10:12,210 --> 00:10:13,540
but I want to check
 with each one of you

301
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:15,980
personally before you
 go out this morning.

302
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:17,100
Jimmy, I'll talk with you first.

303
00:10:17,100 --> 00:10:18,370
-Because you've got
 the most problems.

304
00:10:18,370 --> 00:10:21,320
-The meeting's adjourned.

305
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:24,960
Jimmy, from what you say,
 I'd put these two away

306
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:25,490
for two weeks.

307
00:10:25,490 --> 00:10:25,990
-Whack 'em.

308
00:10:25,990 --> 00:10:26,540
Whack 'em good.

309
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:29,530
-Your time is too valuable to
 be spending on them right now.

310
00:10:29,530 --> 00:10:30,820
-Yeah, mhm.

311
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:34,200
-And then I'd take these
 four in this order.

312
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:36,240
-Uh, boss, will you help
 me with my soapbox racer?

313
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:38,780
-It'll save you time
 in covering them.

314
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:40,450
Now have you got any others
 up in this end of town

315
00:10:40,450 --> 00:10:41,350
when you get up there?

316
00:10:41,350 --> 00:10:42,000
-Uh, no sir.

317
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,360
I don't believe I have.

318
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:44,350
-All right.

319
00:10:44,350 --> 00:10:45,760
-Here's a lollipop, Jimmy.

320
00:10:45,760 --> 00:10:47,960
-I'll give you the names
 of three Chevrolet owners

321
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:49,040
who live up that way.

322
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:50,850
-Crush 'em.

323
00:10:50,850 --> 00:10:55,240
-And all three of them
 should be about ready to buy.

324
00:10:55,240 --> 00:10:57,360
-Meanwhile, Eliot Ness
 and his Untouchables

325
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:58,630
head for a speakeasy in Berwin.

326
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:04,820
[GASPS]

327
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:07,880
-Sintar gets the most sales
 because he's a shape shifter.

328
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:10,050
-Harry, I don't think you
 drove quite far enough

329
00:11:10,050 --> 00:11:12,180
with that prospect before
 you let him take the wheel.

330
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:14,500
-Yes Mr. Warren, but
 he bought the car.

331
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:16,190
-I know, I know,
 but I'm speaking

332
00:11:16,190 --> 00:11:17,980
about your demonstrations
 in general.

333
00:11:17,980 --> 00:11:19,240
-But he bought the flippin' car!

334
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:21,220
-You like to turn the
 wheel over to your prospect

335
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:22,800
pretty early in the
 demonstration ride.

336
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:23,300
-I know, but--

337
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:25,480
-Now, you know, of course
 that when you drive first,

338
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:25,980
-Yeah, but--

339
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:29,040
-The prospect almost invariably
 drives back over the same route

340
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:29,580
that you took.

341
00:11:29,580 --> 00:11:31,200
-Yeah, but he bought the car!

342
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:32,900
-And if you'd driven on
 to that rough stretch

343
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:34,720
up on Summit
 Avenue, you wouldn't

344
00:11:34,720 --> 00:11:35,940
have to direct
 him over it later.

345
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:37,230
-He bought the car.

346
00:11:37,230 --> 00:11:39,270
-That'll give you a chance to
 have him keep his mind more

347
00:11:39,270 --> 00:11:41,220
on the car than on the road.

348
00:11:41,220 --> 00:11:43,310
-Uh, you got a
 nice office, boss.

349
00:11:43,310 --> 00:11:45,420
-It's usually best to--
 I'll go along with you again

350
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:47,510
this morning, Jimmy, on
 these first two calls.

351
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:49,380
-Gee, that'll be
 swell, Mr. Warren.

352
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:51,120
I'll sure appreciate your help.

353
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:53,300
I always learn something
 too, when we go out together.

354
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:55,240
-Yeah, maybe I can kiss your
 butt on the way out, hmm?

355
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:55,740
How about that?

356
00:11:55,740 --> 00:11:56,240
Heh?

357
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:58,030
He he.

358
00:11:58,030 --> 00:11:59,970
-Uh, just gotta push my
 family out of the way.

359
00:11:59,970 --> 00:12:01,410
I kinda live out
 of my car, if you

360
00:12:01,410 --> 00:12:04,730
know what I mean, there, just--

361
00:12:04,730 --> 00:12:07,500
-So, that's when I got into
 commemorative spoon collecting.

362
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:08,000
You?

363
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:12,230
-Why don't you punch it, Jimmy.

364
00:12:12,230 --> 00:12:15,370
I wanna be in Tijuana by
 8 o'clock or you're fired.

365
00:12:15,370 --> 00:12:16,800
-Oh, they're meeting
 with Floyd the Barber.

366
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:19,340
-(IMITATING FLOYD) Oh, oh, the
 car is so wonderful, oh, oh!

367
00:12:22,020 --> 00:12:24,010
-Now listen, I said
 no you two yesterday.

368
00:12:24,010 --> 00:12:25,410
-But ma'am, do you
 know that Chevrolet

369
00:12:25,410 --> 00:12:27,570
has a wonderful
 plan for your life?

370
00:12:27,570 --> 00:12:31,460
-Why'd you bring the greasy guy?

371
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:33,630
-Better see them again tomorrow.

372
00:12:33,630 --> 00:12:35,850
And be sure to have Mrs. Beckham
 into the showroom tonight

373
00:12:35,850 --> 00:12:36,760
with her husband.

374
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:38,070
-OK, I'll sure do it.

375
00:12:38,070 --> 00:12:39,920
Thanks again for your
 help, Mr. Warren.

376
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:41,170
-Uh, may I call you?

377
00:12:41,170 --> 00:12:43,930
I'd like to see
 ya again sometime.

378
00:12:43,930 --> 00:12:48,390
-Are you now, or have you
 ever been, a Ford owner?

379
00:12:48,390 --> 00:12:50,580
-I wouldn't buy a car from
 you if you were the last man

380
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:52,950
on Earth, you sorry sack of--

381
00:12:52,950 --> 00:12:55,180
-That's where your truck
 data book comes in.

382
00:12:55,180 --> 00:12:55,880
-Drug data?

383
00:12:55,880 --> 00:12:58,600
-You'll find the information you
 want on that special equipment

384
00:12:58,600 --> 00:12:59,520
right back here.

385
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:00,650
-Uh, somewheres.

386
00:13:00,650 --> 00:13:03,600
-Now, show this information
 to your prospect, too.

387
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:06,190
Just don't keep glancing at it
 and then telling them about it.

388
00:13:06,190 --> 00:13:07,900
Let them read it.

389
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:13,050
Very often, the printed word is
 a big help in closing the sale.

390
00:13:13,050 --> 00:13:16,410
-Oh, look, it's
 salesman-parent conferences.

391
00:13:16,410 --> 00:13:19,190
-Well, my life is a hollow lie.

392
00:13:19,190 --> 00:13:21,550
-Just too many of them
 on hand now, George.

393
00:13:21,550 --> 00:13:22,780
That's the reason
 we couldn't afford

394
00:13:22,780 --> 00:13:24,300
to offer a higher appraisal.

395
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:26,900
-Yeah, I can see your
 side of the picture.

396
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:28,990
-Yes, but there's a
 good side too, George.

397
00:13:28,990 --> 00:13:32,170
For example, right now we
 haven't a single '39 Plymouth

398
00:13:32,170 --> 00:13:33,610
four-door on the lot.

399
00:13:33,610 --> 00:13:35,360
And that means we can
 afford to make somebody

400
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:36,480
a pretty good offer.

401
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:39,190
-Say, I've got a prospect
 downtown with one of those.

402
00:13:39,190 --> 00:13:40,830
-Not too hot, but I was
 going to get too him

403
00:13:40,830 --> 00:13:42,020
again in the next few days.

404
00:13:42,020 --> 00:13:43,430
-Fine, get him in here today.

405
00:13:43,430 --> 00:13:45,050
And I'm sure we can make
 them an offer that'll

406
00:13:45,050 --> 00:13:46,360
close a deal for you.

407
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:47,270
Besides, we can use--

408
00:13:47,270 --> 00:13:47,960
[GASPS]

409
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:50,200
-Mr. Milton, you can depend
 on what our Mr. Hill here

410
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:51,890
has just told you.

411
00:13:51,890 --> 00:13:53,430
And I'll give you my
 own personal word,

412
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:55,520
too, that if you have any
 trouble along that line,

413
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:56,630
we'll make it good.

414
00:13:56,630 --> 00:13:57,240
[SNICKERING]

415
00:13:57,240 --> 00:13:58,940
-Well, I don't know.

416
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:01,810
The last two cars I bought,
 when they had trouble,

417
00:14:01,810 --> 00:14:03,920
it was always my
 fault, never the car's.

418
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:05,490
-We want you to be
 just as satisfied

419
00:14:05,490 --> 00:14:07,500
with your new Chevrolet
 next year, Mr. Milton,

420
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:08,680
as you will be next week.

421
00:14:08,680 --> 00:14:09,710
-That's exactly right.

422
00:14:09,710 --> 00:14:10,800
-Just buy the car!

423
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:12,830
-We couldn't have afforded to
 make the investment we have

424
00:14:12,830 --> 00:14:15,700
here unless we gave the
 kind of satisfaction that

425
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:19,150
make our customers come
 back to us year after year.

426
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:21,960
We want you to be a regular
 customer, too, Mr. Milton.

427
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:24,570
In fact, we hope you'll buy
 your next ten cars from us.

428
00:14:24,570 --> 00:14:25,310
-Ten cars?

429
00:14:25,310 --> 00:14:26,840
-That's why we'll give
 you the kind of service

430
00:14:26,840 --> 00:14:29,590
it takes on this one to bring
 you back for the next one.

431
00:14:29,590 --> 00:14:31,760
And the next, and
 the next, so on.

432
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:33,530
You see, that's the only
 way we can hope to--

433
00:14:33,530 --> 00:14:37,320
-How can I keep
 this from Gladys?

434
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:40,030
-Adlai Stevenson buys a car.

435
00:14:40,030 --> 00:14:42,040
-Good salesman.

436
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:45,200
-Bad salesman.

437
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:48,180
-(IMITATES JIMMY DURANTE)
 Inka dinka do, ha ha ha ha ha!

438
00:14:48,180 --> 00:14:49,770
-Now, if you want to
 see your daughter again,

439
00:14:49,770 --> 00:14:51,010
you'll do what I--

440
00:14:51,010 --> 00:14:52,160
-And now let us pray.

441
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:53,610
Lord!

442
00:14:53,610 --> 00:14:55,540
-Huh?

443
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:56,560
-You could hurt me?

444
00:15:01,110 --> 00:15:02,080
-(ANGRILY) Would you sign it?

445
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:02,770
Just sign it!

446
00:15:02,770 --> 00:15:04,400
Sign it!

447
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:08,920
-Well, can I keep the pen?

448
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:09,970
-I was kidding.

449
00:15:09,970 --> 00:15:13,260
I was going to sign when I
 walked in here, ha, you nuts.

450
00:15:13,260 --> 00:15:17,720
-Christy ahead at the break,
 Dunn and Stewart close behind.

451
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:18,220
-Huh?

452
00:15:18,220 --> 00:15:20,210
-Believe me, leadership pays.

453
00:15:20,210 --> 00:15:21,340
-Is he talking to us?

454
00:15:21,340 --> 00:15:21,870
-Mhm.

455
00:15:21,870 --> 00:15:22,370
-Yeah.

456
00:15:22,370 --> 00:15:23,350
-First, hire good men.

457
00:15:23,350 --> 00:15:24,310
-Make sure they're clean.

458
00:15:24,310 --> 00:15:26,660
-Then see that they're well
 trained on their own product

459
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:29,290
and on competitive products.

460
00:15:29,290 --> 00:15:30,230
Second,

461
00:15:30,230 --> 00:15:30,910
-Check their teeth.

462
00:15:30,910 --> 00:15:32,370
-Be sure they have
 all the equipment

463
00:15:32,370 --> 00:15:36,260
and selling helps they need
 and know how to use them.

464
00:15:36,260 --> 00:15:36,760
-Fifth--

465
00:15:36,760 --> 00:15:37,260
-Third,

466
00:15:37,260 --> 00:15:37,940
-Oh, right, third.

467
00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:39,400
-Help them plan
 their work to make

468
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:42,600
the most effective
 use of their time.

469
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:43,100
Fourth,

470
00:15:43,100 --> 00:15:44,550
-Live at home until you're 40.

471
00:15:44,550 --> 00:15:46,760
-Stay in close touch
 with every man.

472
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:48,550
Know what each one is doing.

473
00:15:48,550 --> 00:15:49,390
Work with them.

474
00:15:49,390 --> 00:15:50,430
-Name names.

475
00:15:50,430 --> 00:15:53,010
-And fifth, keep up
 their enthusiasm.

476
00:15:53,010 --> 00:15:53,660
-En-too-zee-az-um.

477
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:56,490
-Encourage every man every day.

478
00:15:56,490 --> 00:15:57,800
-Give 'em backrubs.

479
00:15:57,800 --> 00:15:58,850
-And never forget,

480
00:15:58,850 --> 00:15:59,740
-I love you.

481
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:04,270
-As I did, that people
 who represent you--

482
00:16:04,270 --> 00:16:05,150
-It's Dr. Giggles!

483
00:16:05,150 --> 00:16:05,930
-Need your help.

484
00:16:05,930 --> 00:16:06,430
-Goodnight.

485
00:16:06,430 --> 00:16:08,780
Stay pink, soft and oily.

486
00:16:08,780 --> 00:16:10,810
-(SINGING) We're hired!

487
00:16:10,810 --> 00:16:11,300
-Hired!

488
00:16:26,590 --> 00:16:30,340
-Yes, it's Manos,
 the Hands of Fate.

489
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:35,510
-You know, this is the slowest
 car chase scene I've ever seen.

490
00:16:35,510 --> 00:16:37,410
-Like father, like son.

491
00:16:37,410 --> 00:16:38,750
Think about it, won't you?

492
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:46,020
-This is where you
 get out, honey.

493
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:47,010
-We're almost there, honey.

494
00:16:47,010 --> 00:16:49,490
Just a little while longer
 and your vacation starts.

495
00:16:49,490 --> 00:16:52,050
-I've been cold, Mother.

496
00:16:52,050 --> 00:16:55,450
-Visit beautiful ground zero.

497
00:16:55,450 --> 00:16:57,010
-Hey, look.

498
00:16:57,010 --> 00:16:58,550
Poodle bites, poodle chews.

499
00:16:58,550 --> 00:17:00,090
-(IN CHILD'S VOICE) His
 heartbeat's irregular.

500
00:17:03,460 --> 00:17:04,960
-We should be pretty
 close right now.

501
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:07,080
The agent said it was about
 12 miles from Highway 10

502
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:09,330
and that was Highway
 10 back there.

503
00:17:09,330 --> 00:17:10,520
-But you know, we
 should've asked

504
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:12,890
for better directions
 at the last gas station.

505
00:17:12,890 --> 00:17:14,910
-Listen, I've never
 gotten us lost before.

506
00:17:14,910 --> 00:17:15,600
-Have I?

507
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:18,450
Honey?

508
00:17:18,450 --> 00:17:20,850
-Mommy, I'm cold.

509
00:17:20,850 --> 00:17:22,530
-(IN CHILD'S VOICE)My core
 temperature's dropped.

510
00:17:22,530 --> 00:17:23,420
-OK, Debbie.

511
00:17:23,420 --> 00:17:24,740
I'll put the top up.

512
00:17:24,740 --> 00:17:25,630
-Nevermind, Mike.

513
00:17:25,630 --> 00:17:27,440
Debbie can come
 up sit between us.

514
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:29,570
-You can have the top up
 or a birthday present.

515
00:17:29,570 --> 00:17:30,760
It's your choice, honey.

516
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:35,760
-Ah, what do you
 want, a hug next?

517
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:36,780
Jeez, kids.

518
00:17:36,780 --> 00:17:38,060
-Cousin Itt!

519
00:17:38,060 --> 00:17:38,990
